{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Fat of cattle and the milk of sheep.</b> This was in the days of Solomon, as is written (I Kings 4): \"ten fattened oxen and twenty pasture-fed cattle and a hundred sheep.\"" "<b>With the fat of lambs.</b> This was in the days of the ten tribes, as is written (Amos 6): \"and eat lambs from the flock.\"" "<b>The fat of kidneys of wheat.</b> This was in the days of Solomon, as is written (I Kings 5): \"And Solomon's provisions were…\"" "<b>And the blood of grapes you drink, wine.</b> In the days of the ten tribes — \"those who drink from wine-bowls\" (Amos 6)." "<b>Fat of cattle.</b> It is the suet absorbed from upon the milk." "<b>And the fat of sheep.</b> Fat of sheep — and when it is joined it is pointed חֲלֵב, as in ״בַּחֲלֵב אִמּוֹ״ (Exodus 23)." "<b>Rams.</b> Sheep." "<b>And rams.</b> As its meaning." "<b>Bulls of Bashan.</b> They were fat." "<b>Fat of wheat.</b> Wheats that are as fat as kidney-fat and as coarse as כּוּלְיָא." "<b>And the blood of grapes.</b> You will be drinking good wine and tasting choice wine." "<b>חמר.</b> Wine in Aramaic is חֲמַר — this is not a noun but a term meaning superior in taste; ויי\"נוש בְּלַעַ\"ז. And there is further to interpret these two verses after the Targum of Onkelos: ״אַשְׁרִינוּן עַל תָּקְפֵּי אַרְעָא״ and so forth."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {9 "כּוּלְיָא: a coarse food or grain product; possibly related to Aramaic כּוּלְיָא meaning a type of large bean or porridge.", 11 "The la'az ויי\"נוש is apparently Old French vin musc, 'muscadine/muscat wine', matching Rashi's gloss of חמר as wine superior in taste."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:49:22.310236292Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}